a delay in the delivery of oxygen to a  hospital in the city of peshawar  is being blamed for the deaths of  several coronavirus patients  they'd relied on oxygen to stay alive at  the kaiba teaching hospital  but with no backup supply they died  shortly  after it was cut the minister of health  of the pashtun province  tweeted a preliminary report from the  hospital that showed failures  in many areas it cites a lack of oxygen  for several months  and cylinders not being completely  filled it says this went unnoticed  unsupervised and unchecked staff are  also absent at the hospital's oxygen  plant  transparency compromise there will be no  compromise  on transparency action should be tough  and fair and bring back the credibility  of the hospital to our masses  we tried our best in making the report  public in as little time as possible  this sad incident will help put us in  the right direction for the future  strategy  of health care although staff members  have been suspended  some analysts say this reflects a  government failure to prepare for the  pandemic  this incident is a  reflects carelessness callousness  criminal negligence as such disaster  management  one of the essentials is preparedness  and then mitigation in that case  i would say uh the government of hebrew  in particular and the government of  pakistan  have visibly failed as for preparedness  is concerned  the world health organization in  november praised pakistan for its fight  against the pandemic  saying it had taken appropriate measures  to control infections in maine june  it worked with the country to set up  labs and to be able to test 50  000 people a day but so far only around  two percent of pakistan's population of  around 220 million people  has been tested for the virus back in  bashawa  as an investigation is underway many  want to know why there were so many  failures  and how they all seem to go unnoticed  law about a manly al jazeera
 

